    {
      "name": "Crash attribution",
      "stylistic_components": [
        {
          "id": "c-h",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Which journeys end in a crash?",
            "variant": "h2"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 0,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 3
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-p",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Everything on this tab is read straight off the crash report itself, by a Ripsaw script inspecting the report's custom fields — not assembled from a flow. That distinction matters: a crash cannot be the terminal node of a Sankey on iOS, so the journey has to arrive already attached to the report. The app maintains a 5-deep shift register of screens as global fields, and global fields ride onto the crash report, so every crash carries the path that led to it.",
            "variant": "p"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 3,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-h1",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Start here — where did they come from?",
            "variant": "h3"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 9,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 3
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-p1",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "The screen immediately before the one that crashed. If a single screen dominates this table, that transition is where to look first — it is the last thing the user did before the app died.",
            "variant": "p"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 12,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 4
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-h2",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "The path, and where it ended",
            "variant": "h3"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 28,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 3
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-p2",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Left: the last three screens joined, so the most common route into a crash is readable as one row. Right: the screen the crash actually happened on. Three deep rather than the full five on purpose — as a chart dimension the full path goes high-cardinality and silently collapses into an 'other' bucket. All five are still readable per-report under Custom Fields.",
            "variant": "p"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 31,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-h3",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Which crashes can carry a path at all?",
            "variant": "h3"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 53,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 3
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "c-p3",
          "text_component": {
            "text": "Not every crash class can. Kinds caught in-process carry the global fields; kinds the OS reports on the next launch arrive in a fresh process where no fields are set yet, and attribute as 'unknown'. The left table splits screen attribution by error kind so you can see which is which — 'unknown' concentrated on one error kind is expected behaviour, not a broken register.",
            "variant": "p"
          },
          "dashboard_layout_settings": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 56,
            "column_span": 12,
            "row_span": 6
          }
        }
      ],
